Subject: A stage found in the cache waits for no compiler; what a pipeline's making cost is said

compileStage took the one compiler before it looked in the stage cache. A
render pipeline whose two stages were both on disk -- half a millisecond of
reading -- then stood behind whatever another thread was compiling. On the
phone that other thread is the plug-in's, compiling the uber shader for a
configuration in the background (0.45 to 1.1 s each): in the frame traces
pipelines made from cached stages take 173, 415, 423, 431, 433 ms each, three
of them one after another in a frame of 1.3 s, and the first opening of the app
switcher spends 173 of its 228 ms in one.

The cache and its seeds are now looked in first, with no lock; only a
compilation takes the compiler, and looks once more once it has it, for the
stage another thread may have compiled while it waited. Nothing of what is
compiled changes.

CompileTimes (hakios_metal_compile_times) says, for the calling thread since it
last asked, the stages asked for, those read from the cache and those compiled,
and the time spent waiting for the compiler, reading and compiling. The
plug-in's linked line carries it for every pipeline; the frame trace's pipe
line gains stages, cached, compiled, wait_ms, load_ms and cc_ms, and its commit
line what the driver's own work cost while the buffer was being encoded:
textures made, textures over an IOSurface, buffers, uploads, pipelines, and the
copies of surfaces the GPU cannot read where they lie, each as
count/milliseconds/megabytes.

The compiler is not in the host build: the change is compiled for iOS with the
bundle; the host's stub answers zeros.


